---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/RealestateLayout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="GTA Home Seller's Guide | Cory Wong, Real Estate Agent | Trustwell Realty"
  description="A comprehensive guide to selling a home in the Greater Toronto Area. Preparation, pricing strategy, marketing, navigating offers, and the closing process."
  canonicalPath="/resources/seller-guide"
>
  <!-- Page Hero -->
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <h1 class="page-hero__title">The GTA Home Seller's Guide</h1>
      <p class="page-hero__byline">By Cory Wong, Real Estate Agent</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Guide Content -->
  <article class="guide">
    <div class="container content-narrow">

      <!-- Table of Contents -->
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
        <h2 class="toc__title">In This Guide</h2>
        <ol class="toc__list">
          <li><a href="#preparing">Preparing Your Home</a></li>
          <li><a href="#pricing">Pricing Strategy</a></li>
          <li><a href="#marketing">Marketing Your Property</a></li>
          <li><a href="#showings-and-offers">Showings, Offers, and Negotiating</a></li>
          <li><a href="#conditional-vs-firm">Conditional vs. Firm Offers</a></li>
          <li><a href="#closing">The Closing Process</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <!-- Section 1: Preparing Your Home -->
      <section id="preparing" class="guide__section">
        <h2 class="guide__section-title">1. Preparing Your Home</h2>
        <p>
          How you prepare your home before it goes on the market directly affects how
          quickly it sells and for how much. The goal isn't perfection &mdash; it's
          presenting the property in its best realistic light so buyers can see themselves
          living there.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Declutter and Depersonalize</h3>
        <p>
          Remove excess furniture, personal photos, and collections that make rooms feel
          smaller or distract from the space itself. Buyers need to be able to imagine their
          own lives in the home. Rent a storage unit if necessary &mdash; it's one of the
          most cost-effective things a seller can do.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Repairs That Matter</h3>
        <p>
          Focus on the details buyers notice: leaking faucets, cracked tiles, peeling paint,
          sticking doors, burned-out light bulbs, stained grout. These small items create a
          cumulative impression of deferred maintenance. Addressing them costs relatively
          little and signals that the home has been well cared for.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Staging</h3>
        <p>
          Professional staging isn't always necessary. If you're living in the home and it's
          well-furnished and clean, strategic adjustments (rearranging furniture, adding a
          few accessories, improving lighting) may be enough. Vacant properties benefit most
          from professional staging &mdash; empty rooms photograph poorly and make it difficult
          for buyers to gauge scale and layout.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">What NOT to Spend On</h3>
        <p>
          A full kitchen renovation rarely recovers its cost at resale. The same applies to
          major bathroom overhauls, high-end landscaping projects, and other significant
          renovations done solely to sell. The exception is if a space is genuinely dated or
          damaged to the point where it's actively deterring buyers &mdash; but even then,
          a targeted refresh (new hardware, paint, updated fixtures) is usually more effective
          than a gut renovation.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Pricing Strategy -->
      <section id="pricing" class="guide__section">
        <h2 class="guide__section-title">2. Pricing Strategy</h2>
        <p>
          Pricing is the single most important decision in the selling process. An accurate
          list price generates interest, drives showings, and positions you for strong offers.
          A mispriced listing &mdash; in either direction &mdash; costs you money.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Comparative Market Analysis (CMA)</h3>
        <p>
          A CMA is the foundation of pricing. It examines recent sales (ideally within the
          last 30 to 90 days) of comparable properties in your area, along with active
          listings (your current competition) and expired listings (properties that failed to
          sell, often because of price). Your agent should walk you through the CMA in detail
          and explain how each comparable supports the recommended price range.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">List Price vs. Hold-Back Strategy</h3>
        <p>
          Some sellers choose to list below market value and hold back offers to a specific
          date, hoping to generate multiple offers and drive the price above asking. This
          strategy can work in a seller's market when demand is strong and inventory is low.
          In a balanced or buyer's market, it can backfire &mdash; you may receive only one
          or two offers at or near your (already discounted) list price, leaving you worse off
          than if you had listed at fair market value from the start.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">The Cost of Overpricing</h3>
        <p>
          Overpriced listings accumulate days on market (DOM), and experienced buyers and
          their agents track this metric closely. A property that sits signals a problem &mdash;
          whether it's price, condition, or both. The longer it sits, the weaker your
          negotiating position becomes. Studies consistently show that price reductions after
          an extended listing period result in a lower final sale price than if the home had
          been priced correctly from the beginning.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Marketing -->
      <section id="marketing" class="guide__section">
        <h2 class="guide__section-title">3. Marketing Your Property</h2>
        <p>
          Effective marketing ensures your listing reaches the right buyers and presents
          your home in the best possible light. In today's market, buyers start their search
          online, and first impressions are formed from photos and listing descriptions before
          a showing is ever booked.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Professional Photography</h3>
        <p>
          Professional real estate photography is standard practice, not a luxury. Listings
          with professional photos receive significantly more views and generate more showing
          requests than those with amateur images. Wide-angle lenses, proper lighting, and
          careful composition make rooms appear true to life while showcasing the property's
          best features.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">MLS and Syndication</h3>
        <p>
          Your listing will be entered into the TRREB MLS system, which syndicates to
          Realtor.ca, Zillow, and other consumer-facing real estate portals. This ensures
          maximum exposure to active buyers and their agents across the GTA and beyond.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Listing Description</h3>
        <p>
          The written description matters more than most sellers realize. A well-crafted
          description highlights what makes the property distinctive, provides context that
          photos can't capture (neighbourhood feel, walkability, natural light at different
          times of day), and gives buyers a reason to book a showing. Cory's writing
          background ensures that every listing description is clear, accurate, and
          compelling &mdash; without the hyperbole that makes buyers skeptical.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Digital Marketing</h3>
        <p>
          Beyond MLS, your listing is promoted through targeted social media campaigns,
          email distribution to active buyer clients and cooperating agents, and Cory's
          professional network. The goal is to put your property in front of buyers who are
          actively searching in your area and price range.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: Showings, Offers, Negotiating -->
      <section id="showings-and-offers" class="guide__section">
        <h2 class="guide__section-title">4. Showings, Offers, and Negotiating</h2>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Showing Logistics</h3>
        <p>
          Showings are typically arranged through your agent and can be managed via lockbox
          (allowing agents to show without you present) or by scheduled appointment. Lockbox
          showings maximize accessibility and generate more traffic, which is why most agents
          recommend them. Keep the home show-ready at all times during the active listing
          period &mdash; the most interested buyers often request showings on short notice.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Reviewing Offers</h3>
        <p>
          When an offer comes in, your agent will review it with you in detail. Price is
          important, but it's not the only factor. A complete evaluation considers:
        </p>
        <ul class="guide__list">
          <li><strong>Price:</strong> How it compares to your list price and recent comparable sales</li>
          <li><strong>Conditions:</strong> Whether the offer is conditional on financing, inspection, or other factors &mdash; and how long the conditional periods are</li>
          <li><strong>Deposit:</strong> The size of the deposit as a signal of buyer commitment (larger deposits indicate stronger intent)</li>
          <li><strong>Closing date:</strong> Whether it aligns with your timeline and moving plans</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Multiple Offers</h3>
        <p>
          If you receive multiple offers, your agent will notify all registered buyer agents
          that a multiple-offer situation exists. You can choose to accept, reject, or counter
          any offer. Offer night requires careful preparation: your agent should have a clear
          strategy for evaluating and responding to offers, and you should be available to
          make decisions in real time.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Negotiation</h3>
        <p>
          Whether you're dealing with a single offer or multiple, negotiation is about more
          than price. Your agent's role is to understand the buyer's position, identify
          leverage points, and structure a deal that meets your priorities while keeping the
          transaction together. Good negotiation is methodical, not emotional.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5: Conditional vs. Firm -->
      <section id="conditional-vs-firm" class="guide__section">
        <h2 class="guide__section-title">5. Conditional vs. Firm Offers</h2>
        <p>
          Understanding the difference between conditional and firm offers &mdash; and how
          to evaluate them &mdash; is one of the most important aspects of the selling process.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Common Conditions</h3>
        <ul class="guide__list">
          <li><strong>Financing:</strong> The buyer's offer is conditional on securing mortgage approval for the specific property. Typical conditional period is 5 to 10 business days.</li>
          <li><strong>Home inspection:</strong> The buyer has the right to conduct a professional inspection and may renegotiate or withdraw based on findings.</li>
          <li><strong>Status certificate review (condos):</strong> The buyer's lawyer reviews the condo corporation's financial and legal documents. This typically requires 10 business days.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Evaluating Conditional Offers</h3>
        <p>
          A conditional offer carries risk: the buyer can walk away during the conditional
          period if conditions aren't satisfied. This means your property is effectively off
          the market while you wait. When evaluating a conditional offer, consider the buyer's
          financial situation (are they pre-approved? how strong is their lender?), the length
          of the conditional period, and whether the conditions are reasonable.
        </p>
        <p>
          In some situations, a firm offer at a lower price may be worth more than a
          conditional offer at a higher price. A firm offer gives you certainty &mdash; the
          deal is binding, and barring exceptional circumstances, it will close. Your agent
          should help you assess this trade-off based on the specifics of each offer and
          current market conditions.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Irrevocability Periods</h3>
        <p>
          Every offer includes an irrevocability period &mdash; the window during which the
          offer remains open for acceptance. Once the irrevocability expires, the buyer can
          withdraw the offer. If you're negotiating a counter-offer, timing matters: you need
          to respond before the irrevocability lapses.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6: Closing Process -->
      <section id="closing" class="guide__section">
        <h2 class="guide__section-title">6. The Closing Process</h2>
        <p>
          Once you have a firm deal, the closing process is largely handled by your lawyer.
          Here's what happens between acceptance and closing day.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Your Lawyer's Role</h3>
        <p>
          Your lawyer handles the legal transfer of title, coordinates with the buyer's
          lawyer, discharges any existing mortgage on the property, and prepares the statement
          of adjustments. If you have a mortgage, your lawyer will arrange the discharge with
          your lender, which can take several weeks &mdash; so it's important to notify them
          early.
        </p>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Adjustments</h3>
        <p>
          The statement of adjustments accounts for prepaid expenses that need to be prorated
          between you and the buyer. Common adjustments include:
        </p>
        <ul class="guide__list">
          <li>Property taxes (if you've prepaid taxes beyond the closing date, you'll be credited)</li>
          <li>Condo fees (if applicable, prorated from the closing date)</li>
          <li>Utility costs (depending on what's included in the sale)</li>
          <li>Rental contracts (hot water tank, HVAC rentals that transfer to the buyer)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Seller Costs at Closing</h3>
        <ul class="guide__list">
          <li><strong>Real estate commission:</strong> As outlined in your listing agreement</li>
          <li><strong>Legal fees:</strong> Typically $1,000 to $2,000 for the seller's side, including disbursements</li>
          <li><strong>Mortgage discharge fee:</strong> Your lender may charge a fee to discharge your existing mortgage (and potentially a prepayment penalty if you're breaking a fixed-rate term)</li>
          <li><strong>No land transfer tax:</strong> Sellers do not pay land transfer tax &mdash; that's the buyer's cost</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="guide__subsection-title">Closing Day</h3>
        <p>
          On closing day, your lawyer will confirm that funds have been received from the
          buyer's lawyer, the title has been transferred, and the mortgage (if applicable) has
          been discharged. You'll hand over the keys &mdash; either through your agent or as
          arranged with the buyer. The sale proceeds, less outstanding mortgage balance,
          commission, legal fees, and adjustments, will be deposited into your account,
          typically by the end of the business day.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- CTA -->
      <div class="guide__cta">
        <h2 class="guide__cta-title">Thinking About Selling?</h2>
        <p class="guide__cta-text">
          Every home and every market is different. If you're considering selling your
          property in the GTA, book a consultation to discuss your specific situation,
          get a market assessment, and understand your options.
        </p>
        <a href="/contact" class="cta cta--primary">Book a Consultation</a>
      </div>

      <!-- Compliance -->
      <div class="guide__compliance">
        <p>
          Cory Wong, Real Estate Agent. Real estate services provided through Trustwell
          Realty Inc., Brokerage. This guide is intended for general informational purposes
          only and does not constitute legal, financial, or professional advice. Information
          is believed to be accurate as of early 2026 but is subject to change. Consult
          with qualified professionals for advice specific to your situation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Newsletter -->
  <section class="newsletter">
    <div class="container content-narrow">
      <h2 class="newsletter__heading">Stay Informed</h2>
      <p class="newsletter__text">
        Get monthly market data, neighbourhood insights, and practical advice for buyers
        and sellers across the Greater Toronto Area.
      </p>
      <form id="newsletter-form" class="newsletter__form" novalidate>
        <input
          type="email"
          id="newsletter-email"
          name="email"
          placeholder="you@example.com"
          required
          autocomplete="email"
          class="newsletter__input"
        />
        <button type="submit" class="cta cta--primary newsletter__btn">Subscribe</button>
      </form>
      <p id="newsletter-form-status" class="form-status" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<script>
  const newsletterForm = document.getElementById('newsletter-form') as HTMLFormElement | null;
  const newsletterStatus = document.getElementById('newsletter-form-status');

  if (newsletterForm && newsletterStatus) {
    newsletterForm.addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      newsletterStatus.textContent = '';
      newsletterStatus.className = 'form-status';

      const formData = new FormData(newsletterForm);
      const payload = {
        type: 'newsletter',
        email: formData.get('email'),
      };

      try {
        const res = await fetch('/api/lead', {
          method: 'POST',
          headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
          body: JSON.stringify(payload),
        });

        if (res.ok) {
          newsletterStatus.textContent = 'You\'re subscribed! Watch your inbox for the next report.';
          newsletterStatus.classList.add('form-status--success');
          newsletterForm.reset();
        } else {
          throw new Error('Request failed');
        }
      } catch {
        newsletterStatus.textContent = 'Something went wrong. Please try again.';
        newsletterStatus.classList.add('form-status--error');
      }
    });
  }
</script>

